  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Gusto Alternative",
    "body": "Picking a Gusto alternative starts with the number of people paid each month, because every published price on this page adds a per-person fee to a monthly base fee. For a 10-person team, Gusto Simple costs $49 + 10 x $6 = $109 a month, or $1,308 a year. The same team costs $80 + 10 x $12 = $200 a month ($2,400 a year) on Gusto Plus and $180 + 10 x $22 = $400 a month ($4,800 a year) on Gusto Premium.\n\nAsk five questions of each plan before comparing totals.\n\n- How many people are paid each month, and what do the base fee and the per-person fee add up to?\n- Does the plan file payroll taxes? Patriot Basic Payroll leaves tax filing to the employer, while Patriot Full Service, Square full-service payroll and Gusto Simple list tax filings.\n- Does payroll run in more than one state? Gusto Simple runs basic payroll in one state, multi-state payroll starts on Gusto Plus, and Justworks Payroll also lists multi-state payroll.\n- Are contractors paid? Gusto lists domestic contractor payments on all three plans, Patriot pays contractors in payroll, Square sells contractor-only payroll and Justworks lists contractor payments.\n- Is a PEO wanted? Justworks publishes PEO prices, and Paychex Flex PEO is sold separately from Paychex Flex.\n\nPaychex Flex and ADP RUN publish no prices, so the same arithmetic works for them only once a quote arrives. If a quote comes back as a base fee plus a per-person fee, multiply the per-person fee by headcount, add the base fee and set the total beside Gusto Simple's $109 for 10 people."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "OnPay Payroll Essentials at Gusto Simple's Price",
      "body": "OnPay Payroll Essentials costs $49 a month plus $6 per worker, the same published price as Gusto Simple. For a 10-person team, both plans come to $109 a month. OnPay includes W-2s, 1099s and year-end filings on every plan and says it has no hidden fees.\n\nOnPay sells HR and time tracking as separate modules. HR costs $15 base plus $2 per worker, and Time Tracking alone costs $15 base plus $4 per worker. HR plus Time Tracking costs $21 base plus $4 per worker.\n\nOnPay also lists two bundles that include Payroll Essentials. Bundled HR costs $71 base plus $1 per worker, and bundled HR plus Time Tracking costs $81 base plus $4 per worker.\n\nOnPay advertises white-glove setup and free data migration, so moving payroll records into OnPay carries no published fee. Benefits come with free integration and admin, and a business pays only the plan premiums for health insurance, workers' comp and 401(k). The OnPay pricing page also promotes a partner program that pays accounting firms up to $10,000 when they switch clients.\n\nOnPay fits businesses that want Gusto Simple's price with HR or time tracking added as modules. Because the two payroll plans list the same price, the comparison turns on plan contents. Gusto Simple lists unlimited payrolls, basic paid time off (PTO) policies, holiday pay and domestic contractor payments, while OnPay lists year-end filings and free data migration on all plans."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Patriot Software Basic and Full Service Payroll",
      "body": "Patriot Software publishes the lowest base fee on this page, with Basic Payroll at $17 a month plus $4 per worker paid. For 10 workers, Basic Payroll costs $17 + 10 x $4 = $57 a month, or $684 a year. That is $109 - $57 = $52 a month less than Gusto Simple, which adds up to $624 a year.\n\nPatriot Basic Payroll leaves tax filing to the employer. Full Service Payroll costs $37 a month plus $5 per worker paid and adds federal, state and local tax filings and deposits, plus year-end payroll tax filings at no additional fee. For 10 workers, Full Service costs $37 + 10 x $5 = $87 a month ($1,044 a year), which is $22 a month and $264 a year below Gusto Simple.\n\nPatriot lists unlimited payrolls, free direct deposit, a free employee portal, auto payroll, contractor payments inside payroll and free health benefit integration. Time & Attendance and HR Software are add-ons at $6 a month plus $2 per employee each. In any month a business runs no payroll, Patriot charges only the base price.\n\nThe Patriot page showed a promotion of 30 days free plus 50% off for 3 months, which brings Basic to $8.50 plus $2 during those months. The worked figures above use the regular price. Patriot also sells accounting software at $20 and $30 a month.\n\nPatriot Software fits businesses that want the lowest published payroll fee and can decide who files payroll taxes. A business that files its own taxes can run Basic Payroll, and a business that wants filings handled can pick Full Service at $37 plus $5."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Square Payroll for Square Point of Sale Users",
      "body": "Square Payroll's full-service plan costs $35 a month plus $6 a month per person paid. For a 10-person team, that is $35 + 10 x $6 = $95 a month, or $1,140 a year. Square full-service payroll costs $14 a month less than Gusto Simple, which is $168 a year.\n\nSquare Payroll connects with Square point of sale, so timecards and tips import straight into payroll. Square Payroll also syncs with QuickBooks Online accounting. The full-service plan lists unlimited pay runs each month, automated tax filings and simple onboarding.\n\nContractor-only payroll costs $6 a month per person paid, and Square lists no base fee for it. A business paying 10 contractors and no employees pays 10 x $6 = $60 a month. Mailed paper W-2s or 1099s are optional at an annual fee of $3 per employee or contractor.\n\nSquare lists no commitment or long-term contract on payroll. Square also lists health insurance and a 401(k) option, and the payroll pricing page publishes no prices for either.\n\nSquare Payroll fits businesses that take payments through Square point of sale and want hours and tips to flow into each pay run. A business without Square point of sale still gets the $35 plus $6 price, which sits between Patriot Full Service and Gusto Simple for 10 people."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Justworks Payroll and PEO Plans",
      "body": "Justworks sells a payroll plan at $50 a month plus $8 per employee and two PEO plans priced per employee with no base fee. For 10 employees, Justworks Payroll costs $50 + 10 x $8 = $130 a month, or $1,560 a year. That is $21 a month above Gusto Simple and $70 a month below Gusto Plus, and both Justworks Payroll and Gusto Plus list multi-state payroll.\n\nThe Justworks payroll plan lists contractor payments, off-cycle payments, tips processing, W-2 and 1099 filings and state unemployment insurance administration. It also includes payroll and HR tools, access to time tracking and expert support.\n\nA professional employer organization shares employer responsibilities with the business. Justworks PEO Basic costs $79 per employee per month, or 10 x $79 = $790 a month for 10 people. PEO Basic lists payroll, HR essentials, support and compliance, a 401(k), 24/7 support and an applicant tracking system.\n\nJustworks PEO Plus costs $124 per employee per month, or 10 x $124 = $1,240 a month for 10 people. PEO Plus adds medical, dental and vision administration, health savings account (HSA) and flexible spending account (FSA) options, and mental health and fertility benefits. Workers' comp access is required for both PEO plans.\n\nFor global hiring, the Justworks EOR service costs $599 per employee per month. Justworks fits businesses that want a PEO with published per-employee prices. A 10-person team on PEO Basic pays $790 - $400 = $390 a month more than on Gusto Premium, so the PEO structure is the reason to pay that gap."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Paychex Flex Quote-Based Payroll",
      "body": "Paychex Flex publishes no price, and its payroll page offers Request Pricing and a free quote instead. Paychex groups its solutions for businesses with 1 to 19, 20 to 99 and 100+ employees. Paychex Flex covers payroll, time and attendance, benefits, insurance and HR services.\n\nPaychex says payroll can run in as little as 30 seconds. WISE is the artificial intelligence (AI) built into Paychex Flex. Paychex Pre-check lets employees review their paychecks in advance.\n\nSupport for Paychex Flex runs by phone, chat or email. Paychex describes Paychex Flex as primarily designed for United States (US)-based employees and contractors. Paychex Flex PEO is a separate service from Paychex Flex.\n\nWith no published price, Paychex Flex cannot be placed in the 10-person arithmetic on this page until a quote arrives. Ask the quote to show any base fee and any per-person fee as separate lines, so the total can be set beside Gusto Simple at $109, Gusto Plus at $200 and Gusto Premium at $400.\n\nPaychex Flex fits businesses that want one quoted package spanning payroll, benefits, insurance and HR, with phone, chat or email support. A business that wants a price before any sales contact can use the published plans from Gusto, OnPay, Patriot Software, Square Payroll or Justworks."
    },
    {
      "heading": "ADP RUN for Businesses with 1 to 49 Employees",
      "body": "ADP RUN publishes no price, and its product page shows Get Pricing in place of a rate card. ADP RUN is built for businesses with 1 to 49 employees. Employees can be paid by direct deposit, a Wisely debit card or paper check.\n\nADP RUN also covers recruiting, hiring and onboarding employees. Employees get secure mobile access to their pay and forms.\n\nThe ADP RUN page showed a switch promotion of up to 6 months of free payroll, ending November 26 and subject to terms. Because the promotion ends and no regular price is listed, the published figures on this page give no basis for comparing ADP RUN's cost after those months.\n\nA business with 50 or more employees falls outside the range ADP RUN states. Paychex Flex groups solutions for 20 to 99 and 100+ employees, and the published per-person plans from Gusto, OnPay, Patriot Software, Square Payroll and Justworks list no headcount range.\n\nADP RUN fits businesses with 1 to 49 employees that want a Wisely debit card as a pay option alongside direct deposit and paper checks. Gusto lists direct deposits or printable checks on all three of its plans, so the Wisely card is the pay method ADP RUN lists that Gusto's pricing page does not."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Gusto Is the Better Fit",
    "body": "Gusto fits businesses that want payroll, benefits and HR tools on one account with no contract and free setup. All three Gusto plans list direct deposits or printable checks, payroll approvals, Payroll on AutoPilot, an employee app, an employer mobile app, multiple pay rates and schedules, domestic contractor payments, and expenses and reimbursements. Gusto Simple adds unlimited payrolls, tax filings and payments, basic PTO policies and holiday pay.\n\nGusto Plus fits a mixed workforce across states. Plus adds multi-state payroll, time tracking and scheduling, unlimited next day pay and contractor invoice management, plus workforce cost reports and unlimited custom earning types. For a 10-person team, Plus costs $200 a month, which is $70 more than Justworks Payroll for the same headcount.\n\nGusto Premium adds a named advisor, Premium Account Support, HR guidance and tools, and Gusto Business Compliance, which costs $85 a month per state as an add-on. Premium also includes unlimited same-day and instant pay, project-based rules and costing, unlimited custom admin roles, and 25 invoice payments and bank transfers. Same-day pay costs $90 per payroll and instant pay costs $100 per payroll on the lower plans.\n\nGusto's global contractor payments add-on reaches more than 120 countries with no monthly per-contractor fee, and payments to US-based bank accounts cost $5 each. On benefits, the health insurance broker integration costs $6 a month per eligible employee, and HSA, FSA and commuter accounts share one $200 annual service charge. For life and disability insurance, employees pay only the premiums, with no administration fees.\n\nS corporation owners who want to pay themselves through Gusto are pointed to a Gusto Solo plan, and the Gusto pricing page lists no price for it in our fact check. Gusto cites a 4.6-star average on Capterra from 4,137 reviews and 4.6 on Software Advice.\n\nWho this comparison is not for:\n\n- Businesses that want a price before talking to sales fit poorly with Paychex Flex and ADP RUN, which quote on request. Gusto, OnPay, Patriot Software, Square Payroll and Justworks publish their prices.\n- Businesses that do not want to file payroll taxes fit poorly with Patriot Basic Payroll. Patriot Full Service at $37 plus $5 per worker includes federal, state and local filings.\n- Businesses running payroll in more than one state fit poorly with Gusto Simple, which runs basic payroll in one state. Gusto Plus and Justworks Payroll both list multi-state payroll.\n- Businesses with 50 or more employees fall outside ADP RUN's stated range of 1 to 49. Paychex Flex groups solutions for 20 to 99 and 100+ employees.\n- Businesses that want a PEO to share employer responsibilities fit poorly with any payroll-only plan here. Justworks PEO Basic at $79 and PEO Plus at $124 per employee are the published PEO prices, and Paychex Flex PEO is quoted separately.\n\nWhat would change our answer:\n\n- Gusto moving Simple away from $49 plus $6 per person, which would end the price match with OnPay Payroll Essentials.\n- Gusto adding multi-state payroll to Simple, which would remove the main reason to step up to Plus or to Justworks Payroll.\n- Patriot Software adding tax filings to Basic Payroll at $17 plus $4 per worker.\n- Paychex Flex or ADP RUN publishing a price list, which would let both enter the 10-person arithmetic.\n- Square changing full-service payroll from $35 plus $6 per person, or contractor-only payroll from $6 per person.\n- Justworks adding a base fee to its PEO plans or moving PEO Basic and PEO Plus away from $79 and $124 per employee."
  },
  "verdict": "Patriot Software publishes the lowest payroll price on this page, at $57 a month for 10 workers on Basic Payroll or $87 on Full Service with tax filings included. OnPay Payroll Essentials matches Gusto Simple at $109 a month for 10 people, and Square Payroll comes in at $95 with point-of-sale timecards and tips. Justworks fits businesses that want a PEO at $79 or $124 per employee, while Paychex Flex and ADP RUN fit businesses willing to request a quote.\n\nFor Gusto alternatives, the next step is to count the people paid each month, multiply that number by each per-person fee in the table, add the base fee, and request quotes from Paychex Flex or ADP RUN only if no published plan covers the payroll needed.",
